---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/shopify-email-vs-postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-postscript');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Shopify Email or Postscript for cart recovery?', answer: 'Start with the recovery available in Shopify Messaging. Consider Postscript when a dedicated SMS operation justifies the extra workflow ownership, cost and coordination. Compare the actual store configuration rather than assuming native recovery is email-only.' },
  { question: 'Should email always precede a recovery text?', answer: 'No universal sequence is established here. Choose the channel using eligibility and the shopping problem, then test the proposed sequence. A phone number on an order alone does not establish SMS marketing permission.' },
  { question: 'How should cart and checkout flows interact?', answer: 'Define stage transitions and cancellation conditions explicitly. A shopper progressing to checkout should not keep receiving an obsolete cart-stage offer. Cancellation inside one tool does not establish suppression in another.' },
  { question: 'What needs checking before migration?', answer: 'Inventory native and supplier flows, preserve permission and exclusion records, inspect pending work when pausing the old flow, and verify purchase exits and return links before activating a replacement.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Shopify Email vs Postscript for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Decide whether to keep recovery in Shopify or add a dedicated SMS operation. The practical question is who owns each reminder, its cancellation and the offer the shopper receives.</p>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Establish what native recovery can already do</h2>
        <p>The route keeps the familiar Shopify Email name. Current <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify Messaging documentation</a> lists browse, cart and checkout recovery with email, SMS and WhatsApp. Check availability in your store. Checkout recovery emails do not count toward the email allowance; checkout SMS and WhatsApp are charged. Default marketing subscription rules have an email-only checkout exception; do not extend that exception to text eligibility.</p>
        <p>Keep the native workflow when it reaches the intended audience and your team can maintain the content, exclusions and reporting there. Evaluate Postscript when you have an owner for an SMS program and a specific requirement the current setup cannot meet. Buying a second sender without assigning that ownership creates another queue to reconcile.</p>
        <h2>Translate the shopping stage into a concrete flow</h2>
        <p>Postscript's <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564025-understanding-infinity-testing-in-automation-flows">automation guide</a> distinguishes product-view, add-to-cart and checkout-start events, and describes cancellation on progression or order creation. It also distinguishes cart and checkout return-link tags. These are useful configuration details even if you are not buying its premium Infinity Testing feature; confirm the available actions in your account rather than assuming every feature is included.</p>
        <p>For a cart-stage reminder, decide what should happen when checkout starts. For a checkout reminder, decide what should happen when an order is created. Record those decisions separately from the send delay. A correctly timed message can still be wrong if it refers to the earlier cart after the shopper has moved on.</p>
        <p>Do not assume Postscript cancelling its own cart flow also stops Shopify recovery. Make an ownership sheet for native checkout email, native texts, Postscript cart texts, Postscript checkout texts and welcome promotions. For each active flow, list the event, audience, offer, stop condition and person responsible. If two rows target the same shopper and stage, decide whether one should be paused or whether a tested coordination rule is required.</p>
        <h2>Give each message a job worth interrupting for</h2>
        <p>A delivery-cost objection needs a clear shipping explanation or a service response. A bundle discount missing at checkout needs the offer repaired. A financing failure needs a working payment path. None of those problems is solved merely by shortening a reminder or calling it urgent.</p>
        <p>For a returning shopper with a valid cart, a concise return link may be sufficient. For a first-time shopper unsure of size or delivery, email can carry the relevant detail while a text points to a useful destination. This is a proposed content design, not evidence that a particular order of channels performs better. Avoid artificial scarcity, and check that support can respond to the questions the message invites.</p>
        <p>Plan for unavailable variants and changed carts. If the whole basket is unavailable, another checkout prompt is unhelpful; offer an appropriate stock or service path only when eligible. If one variant sells out, make sure the destination explains it rather than silently promising the original basket. Check mobile links, bundle rules, minimum quantities, subscription selections and discount stacking with the products you actually sell.</p>
        <h2>Proposed acceptance test: cancellation and link semantics</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Use authorized test contacts with email-only and both-channel eligibility. Record the permission state and expected flow before creating events. A stored phone number is not enough to establish marketing permission.</li>
          <li>Add a product without starting checkout. Inspect the observed event and selected flow. Open the cart-stage link and confirm its contents, variant and offer. Then start checkout and check that the intended earlier-stage work is cancelled.</li>
          <li>Repeat checkout abandonment and inspect the checkout-specific destination. Purchase while a message is waiting; inspect cancellation in both systems. Include a repeat visit so the chosen re-entry rule cannot accidentally create overlapping reminders.</li>
          <li>Repeat with an opt-out, an unavailable variant and a message delayed by the configured sending window. Check the eventual destination and eligibility again; a delayed text should not carry an expired promise.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>These tests are proposed, not executed results. Retain event, queue, exclusion and delivery timestamps. An entry event without a send can be a valid exclusion; a successful send with the wrong basket is a failure. Resolve discrepancies before activation instead of adopting an arbitrary universal delay or frequency cap.</p>
        <h2>Use fixed product costs when evaluating an incentive</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $743 basket with a 37% full-price gross margin has $468.09 in product costs and $274.91 gross profit. An 11% code takes $81.73 off revenue. The remaining $661.27 minus unchanged product costs leaves $193.18 gross profit, before payment, fulfillment, returns and messaging costs. The profit reduction is $81.73, not $30.24. Product costs do not shrink just because the selling price does.</p>
        <p>For a smaller hypothetical $153 basket at 54% full-price gross margin, product costs are $70.38. The same 11% offer leaves $136.17 revenue and $65.79 gross profit before other variable costs. This is a budgeting illustration, not a recommended discount cap. Use SKU-level costs, existing promotions and expected returns to decide whether an offer is affordable.</p>
        <p>Request a quote for the SMS destinations, message volume and features you need, including any premium optimization. Add staff time and transition overlap. Compare that incremental cost with keeping the intended workflow native. If using AI-generated variants, review claims and offers, and judge profit as well as attributed purchases: Postscript describes Infinity Testing's optimization target as attributed conversion rate, which is not the same as incremental contribution.</p>
        <h2>Measure the added channel and cut over deliberately</h2>
        <p>If traffic permits, propose a randomized comparison of native recovery alone against the intended additional SMS program. Choose sample size and duration for your traffic and decision; no universal holdout percentage is established here. Keep other promotions comparable, deduplicate orders by Shopify order ID and reconcile refunds, discounts and message costs. Do not add two platforms' attributed revenue and call it incremental recovery.</p>
        <p>Before migration, export the current flow settings and permission history your policy allows you to retain. Preserve opt-outs and exclusions; do not convert all checkout phone numbers into subscribers. Name the cutover owner, pause the superseded automation and inspect what happens to already queued messages before enabling its replacement. Keep a rollback configuration and review the first observed entries and exclusions against the ownership sheet.</p>
        <p>If Shopify keeps checkout email and Postscript takes recovery SMS, document that as a split operation with ongoing campaign coordination. If you later evaluate another lifecycle supplier such as Sequenzy, use the same evidence requirements for events, permissions, exits and costs. Adding an orchestration label does not establish that cross-platform suppression works.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Shopify Email vs Postscript FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
